      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're stuck on your lit review? No problem! Just remember, even when you have five authors, you can't just list them all out like a long, tedious list of names; it's like trying to fit five goblins into a single, tiny bottle. You have to be clever, just like a master thief who knows how to sneak past a guard without making a sound.\n\nFor **APA 7th edition**, if you have five authors, you don't list all five names every single time you cite them. Instead, you list the first three authors, followed by an ellipsis (...), and then the last author. It looks like this: (Smith, Jones, Brown, et al., 2023). It's like saying \"and so on\" to a group of goblins who are too numerous to count; you just point to the first few and imply the rest are there, waiting in the shadows.\n\nNow, how does that differ from **Chicago style**? Chicago is a bit more flexible, like a goblin who can change their shape depending on the mood of the room. In Chicago, you usually list all five authors the first time you cite them in the text, like introducing a whole",
